<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Plot the points from the tables on the same coordinate plane. (Consider using different colors or markings for each set of points so you can tell them apart.) </para>
      <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Coordinate grid with two graphs shown by plotted points."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1817dcc93434ecbd070c0706316edaae60d639c3"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Make a couple of observations about how the two graphs compare. </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <list>
      <item> The \(x\)-intercept of both graphs is at \((0,0)\). </item>
      <item> The graphs seem to be a reflection of each other across the \(y\)-axis. </item>
      <item> The vertices both have a \(y\)-coordinate of -4 and also mirror one another across the \(y\)-axis. </item>
      <item> Each function mirrors itself across a vertical line through its vertex. </item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
